606:
(a) All traveling shows, carnivals, circuses, etc., shall be required to be inspected by the Fire Marshal or his assistants before a permit shall be issued. Before a permit is issued the owner, agent, or person in charge shall submit to the Fire Marshal the following:
(1) A State license as required by the Georgia Safety Fire Commissioner;
(2) A certificate executed by a certified testing laboratory, certifying that the tent, decorative materials and tarpaulins meet the requirements for flame resistance as determined in accordance with both the small scale and large scale tests in NFPA 701, and that such flame resistance is effective for the period specified by the permit;
(3) A site plan of the overall site, emergency roadways, cooking appliances, electrical services or generators, all emergency exits, location of fire hydrants, and the exact location of all animals being housed or kept;
(4) A list of all rides inspected by the Georgia Department of Labor; and
(5) Proof of insurance as required by State law.
(b) Immediately prior to the start of the program, the owner or his authorized agent shall orally notify all attendees concerning the location of the exits to be used in case of fire or emergency.

628:
(a) All land clearing fires shall be pit fires. All pit fires shall be conducted in accordance with the most current Air Curtain Destructor Operating Guide and Procedures as published by the North Georgia Region Environmental Protection Division.
(b) Pit fires shall comply with the following requirements:
(1) Pit fires shall be prohibited in the Downtown Overlay District;
(2) The pit shall be at least 300 feet from any occupied or habitable structure. This distance may be increased at the discretion of the Fire Chief or designee;
(3) The pit shall be at least 1000 feet from any major highway or thoroughfare;
(4) Where a site cannot be excavated to the recommended depth due to high water table and/or subsurface rock, the pit may be constructed aboveground level, subject to the same state standards and procedures for a pit belowground level;
(5) Only wood wastes consisting of trees, logs, large brush, stumps, leaves, and untreated lumber may be burned in the pit;
(6) Leaves, sawdust or other densely packed wood wastes, paper, chemically treated, coated or impregnated wood; or non-wood construction materials shall not be burned. The amount of dirt/soil mixture shall be kept to a minimum;
(7) No more than one air curtain destructor is allowed to be operated within a ten- acre permitted site at one time. The air curtain destructor must be under the supervision of a trained operator at all times during operation. In the event there is a need for multiple burning pits there shall be a minimum of 1,000 feet between each burning pit;
(8) Air curtain destructors shall be able to pass an even, nonturbulent flow of air across the top of the pit. Air curtain destructors with an air duct or manifold that have been altered by bends, dents, or holes and are incapable of providing an even and nonturbulent flow of air across the top of the pit are not permitted;
(9) The air curtain destructor shall be operated in a manner to prevent air, land or water pollution, safety/health hazards, or nuisances;
(10) Tires or other rubber products, plastics, heavy oils or asphaltic base or impregnated materials shall not be used to start or maintain air curtain destructor operations;
(11) No smoke emissions exceeding 40 percent opacity may be produced during operation except for a reasonable period during ignition and charging of the pit;
(12) When the pit is cleared of ash, airborne particulate is to be minimized by wetting or mixing the ashes with dirt;
(13) Only diesel fuel or kerosene may be used as an ignitable liquid accelerant, to ignite pit fires; and
(14) Land clearing fires are permitted only during the hours beginning at 10:00 a.m. and ending at sunset.
(Code 1978, § 8-54; Ord. No. 2024-02, 5-16-2024)

631:
The following rules govern all burning in the City:
(1) The Fire Chief, the designee thereof, or the Bureau of Fire Prevention may temporarily prohibit any outdoor fire when atmospheric conditions or local circumstances make such fire hazardous;
(2) No burning is permitted at night, unless specifically permitted by this chapter. Burning is permitted only during the hours beginning at sunrise and ending at sunset;
(3) Burning of solid waste or household garbage is prohibited;
(4) Burning of any materials that emit a heavy dark smoke including but not limited to, rubber products, oils, roofing material, and petroleum based products is prohibited;
(5) Burning within 20 feet of flammable, combustible or explosive materials, unless otherwise permitted by the Fire Prevention Code, is prohibited;
(6) All fires shall be continuously attended by a competent person until extinguished. No person under the age of 18 years shall be deemed competent;
(7) Tools needed to control the fire must be with the person attending the fire at all times;
(8) There shall be a charged water hose connected to a water supply or other fire extinguishing equipment that will reach beyond the burning pile readily available for use; and
(9) Bonfires shall be continuously attended by the permittee and qualified firefighting personnel and apparatus until such fire is extinguished.

659:
The Fire Code official is authorized to revoke a permit issued under the provisions of this Code when it is found by inspection or otherwise that there has been a false statement or misrepresentation as to the material facts in the application or construction documents on which the permit of approval was based including, but not limited to, any one of the following:
(1) The permit is used for a location or establishment other than that for which it was issued;
(2) The permit is used for a condition or activity other than that listed in the permit;
(3) Conditions and limitations set forth in the permit have been violated;
(4) There have been any false statements or misrepresentations as to the material fact in the application for permit or plans submitted or a condition of the permit;
(5) The permit is used by a different person or firm than the name for which it was issued;
(6) The permittee failed, refused or neglected to comply with orders or notices duly served in accordance with the provisions of this Code within the time provided therein; or
(7) The permit was issued in error or in violation of an ordinance, regulation or this Code.

666:
The Fire Marshal or his assistants may revoke a permit issued under this Code when it is found by inspection or otherwise that any one of the following conditions exist:
(1) The permit is used for a location or establishment other than that for which it was issued;
